#!/bin/bash
# gmpecmfft-slurm -- GPU ECM on one number across a Slurm job array: one
# array task per GPU, each running gmpecmfft batches until a factor is
# found anywhere.
#
#   gmpecmfft-slurm submit -d DIR -n TASKS [-1 B1] [-2 B2] [-c CURVES]
#                          [-s SEED] [-b BATCHES] [-F] [--dry-run] [N]
#                          [-- SBATCH-OPTIONS...]
#   gmpecmfft-slurm status DIR
#   gmpecmfft-slurm task DIR          (what each array task runs)
#
# submit writes the run to DIR (which must be on a filesystem every node
# sees) and submits an array of TASKS tasks, one GPU each. N is read
# from stdin when not given; everything after -- goes to sbatch as is
# (partition, time limit, account, ...); --dry-run prints the sbatch
# command instead of running it. Options as gmpecmfft's; -b is batches
# per task (0: until a factor is found).
#
# The same batches as gmpecmfft-mpi, without a coordinator. Batch b runs
# `gmpecmfft -s SEED+b -c CURVES`; array task t of T runs batches t,
# t+T, t+2T, ... (BATCHES of them, or until a factor turns up when
# BATCHES is 0), so the batches are distinct and any one is reproduced on
# a single GPU with that command.
#
# Stopping goes through DIR, which must be on a filesystem every node
# sees. The first task to find a factor claims it (mkdir DIR/found is
# atomic), writes it to DIR/found/factor, creates DIR/STOP, and cancels
# the rest of the array with scancel: running tasks get SIGTERM, which
# gmpecmfft treats as a stop. Should scancel be unavailable or refused,
# running batches still stop within about a quarter second of DIR/STOP
# appearing (gmpecmfft --stop-file), and tasks check it before each
# batch.
#
# Each task appends one line per batch to DIR/results.TASK: batch, seed,
# curves, exit status, seconds. `status` sums them up.
#
# Use one kind of GPU for the array: tasks run batches of equal size, so
# with a mix the faster GPUs finish their share and then sit idle at the
# end while the slower ones complete theirs.
